# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) and adjust. Every variable here is declared in
# src/config/env.schema.ts — that schema, not this file, is the source of truth.
#
# Every variable here has a default except three — DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET and
# SIGN_IN_CODE_SECRET. The rest are listed anyway so the full surface is
# discoverable without reading the schema.
#
# DATABASE_URL was the first exception, and deliberately so — this file used to be able
# to claim that *every* variable had a default, and that property was worth
# giving up rather than working around. Every other default is a guess whose
# cost is bounded: a wrong port fails loudly and immediately, a wrong log level
# is noise. There is no bounded-cost guess about which database this service
# writes to.
# A localhost fallback would let a production deployment with a missing or
# misspelled variable start up pointing at nothing, report itself healthy, and
# fail one request at a time — instead of refusing to start and saying why.

# One of: development | test | production
# Also selects the log format: `development` pretty-prints, everything else
# emits one JSON object per line.
NODE_ENV=development

# HTTP port the server listens on (1-65535)
PORT=3000

# One of: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent
# `info` is the default and the production floor. Use `debug` locally to see
# rejected (4xx) requests, and `silent` to quieten a test run.
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# Comma-separated origins allowed to call this API from a browser. Empty — the
# default — turns CORS off entirely, which is what a deployment with no browser
# client should have.
#
# Each entry must be a bare origin: scheme, host, optional port. No trailing
# slash, no path. `https://app.example.com/` matches nothing, because that
# trailing slash is not part of what a browser sends; the schema rejects it at
# boot rather than letting you find out from a browser console.
#
# Leave empty unless a browser app on another origin calls this service. A local
# frontend on port 5173 would be:
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
CORS_ORIGINS=

# How long one request may occupy a handler before the caller is answered with a
# 504. Covers the gap the database timeouts below cannot: each of those bounds a
# single statement, so two sequential queries can take twice as long as either,
# and a handler doing no database work was unbounded by anything at all.
#
# 15s rather than the 10s DB_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS + DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS sum to,
# so a request legitimately spending its full database budget is not cut off by
# the limit meant to catch the things that have no budget.
#
# It stops *waiting* and answers; it does not cancel the work. The statement runs
# on until Postgres cancels it and the result is discarded, so this protects the
# caller and the connection it held, not the database. A timeout firing is a bug
# report, not a signal to raise this number.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=15000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED — the only variable in this file with no default. See the note at the
# top for why.
#
# Everything needed to reach Postgres in one string: scheme, credentials, host,
# port, database, and the TLS mode. One string rather than six variables because
# it is what `psql`, `pg_dump` and the migration tooling all read — splitting it
# would give this app one description of the database and every other tool a
# second one to keep in step.
#
# The value below matches `docker-compose.yml`, so the local flow is:
#   docker compose up -d --wait
#
# `sslmode` is mandatory and validated at boot. Two values are accepted:
#
#   disable      No encryption. Correct *only* when the database is on this
#                machine — as it is under docker-compose, where traffic never
#                touches a network.
#   verify-full  Encrypt, verify the certificate chain, and verify the hostname
#                matches. The correct value for anything remote.
#
# `require` is deliberately rejected, and the reason is not obvious: in
# node-postgres it does not mean libpq's "encrypt without verifying". It is a
# deprecated alias for full verification that warns on every connect and is
# scheduled to change meaning. Whichever behaviour you want, name it directly.
#
# A database behind a private CA needs no extra variable — add `sslrootcert` to
# this same string and the driver reads the file:
#   postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/db?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/etc/ssl/ca.pem
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app:app@localhost:5432/app?sslmode=disable

# Connections this instance holds open. The arithmetic that matters:
# DB_POOL_MAX × number of instances must stay below the server's
# `max_connections` (100 by default), leaving headroom for migrations and for a
# human with `psql` during an incident — which is exactly when the pool is also
# at its fullest.
#
# Raising this is rarely the fix for slowness. Postgres runs one OS process per
# connection, so past the point where the server's cores are saturated, more
# connections buy context switching rather than throughput.
DB_POOL_MAX=10

# How long a request waits to be handed a connection before giving up.
#
# Covers two situations that feel different but share this timer: opening a new
# connection, and queueing when all DB_POOL_MAX are busy. The second is the one
# you will actually meet in production, and it fires while the database is
# perfectly healthy — which is why this is named for acquiring a connection
# rather than for connecting to anything.
DB_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Server-side limit on any single statement. Postgres cancels the query when it
# is exceeded, which also releases the locks the query was holding.
#
# A safety net, not a tuning knob: the failure it prevents is one slow query
# holding locks while every other request queues behind it — including, at the
# worst possible moment, a migration. Erring tight is the safer direction, since
# too tight fails one endpoint loudly while too loose degrades everything at once.
DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# How long a single statement may take before its log line is raised from
# `debug` to `warn`. Purely a logging threshold — it never cancels anything.
#
# The early-warning half of a pair whose late half is DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS:
# that one is the cliff, this one is the trend you can watch approach it. Keep
# it well below the timeout, or the warning arrives at the same moment as the
# failure it was meant to precede.
#
# Query lines below the threshold are logged at `debug`, so they are silent at
# the production floor of `info`. Set LOG_LEVEL=debug locally to see every
# statement — including the readiness probe's `SELECT 1` every few seconds.
DB_SLOW_QUERY_MS=500

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED — no default. The HS256 key access tokens are signed with; at least
# 32 characters. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# The value below is for local development only. Never deploy it.
JWT_SECRET=local-development-jwt-secret-change-me-0123456789

# REQUIRED — no default. The HMAC key sign-in codes are hashed under before they
# are stored; at least 32 characters, generated the same way. Separate from
# JWT_SECRET so either can be rotated alone. Local development only.
SIGN_IN_CODE_SECRET=local-development-code-secret-change-me-0123456789

# How long an access token lives, in seconds. Short on purpose: it cannot be
# revoked, so this is how long a stolen one stays good.
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=900

# How long a device stays signed in without using its refresh token, in days.
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=60

# Where sign-in codes go. `log` is the only option today: it writes the code to
# the application log (look for "sign-in code") and sends no email. The service
# refuses to start with it when NODE_ENV=production.
MAILER=log
